  "notes": "German \"hands up\"/hi-NRG Eurodance, produced by DJ Manian and Yanou — whose board is entried separately, so this model is the act and that one is the room. The tempo is the highest artist band in this section, 138-150, which the research ties to the documented post-1992 escalation \"up to 150\". The kit is the entry's: \"hard-clipped kick on all four with a very short tail, layered clap on 2&4, offbeat open hats, and long snare rolls into the drop\", with the \"hands up\" idiom pushing the clap and the kick to the front of the mix — hence the loudest accent tier here at 118-127 and a fully authored roll ramp. The signature the entry calls out is that \"a supersaw lead playing the vocal melody in unison or an octave up\" makes the instrumental hook and the vocal hook the same line, so the countermelody is an octave echo at a 0.9 density; `fillGapsOnly` is left inherited so the doubling answers rather than sits on top of the lead. Loops are 8 bars. The documented cover-version pipeline means the harmony is often pre-existing, which is why the families stay archetypal. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the velocities, the roll ramp and the melody register; the research calls the tempo and drum detail genre-derived.",
